"bliddalhenningpau","last":"Bliddal","name":"Bliddal, Henning","rank":21,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels Steen","key":"kroghnielssteenpau","last":"Krogh","name":"Krogh, Niels Steen","rank":22,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Torkell","key":"ellingsentorkellpau","last":"Ellingsen","locid":"12189359-9c0f-4bf1-8b75-be844c75b85b","name":"Ellingsen, Torkell","orcid":"0000-0003-0426-4962","rank":23,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Agnete H.","key":"nielsenagnetehpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Agnete H.","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lone","key":"baldinglonepau","last":"Balding","name":"Balding, Lone","rank":25,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne Grethe","key":"jurikannegrethepau","last":"Jurik","name":"Jurik, Anne Grethe","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik S.","key":"thomsenhenrikspau","last":"Thomsen","name":"Thomsen, Henrik S.","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikkel","key":"østergaardmikkelpau","last":"Østergaard","name":"Østergaard, Mikkel","rank":28,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30721294"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1001/jama.2018.21362","issn":["00987484","15383598"],"issn_org":["00987484"],"issue":"5","journal":"JAMA","pages":"461-472","publisher":"American Medical Association","status":"p","title":"JAMA","volume":"321"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85061060865"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Private Research","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","North Denmark Region","Other Companies","Private Research","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","zitelab aps"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-24c84b56-8be8-4458-86d3-f780686bc48b"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Effect of magnetic resonance imaging vs conventional treat-to-target strategies on disease activity remission and radiographic progression in rheumatoid arthritis the IMAGINE-RA randomized clinical trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-801786","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Importance: Whether using magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) to guide treatment in patients with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) improves disease activity and slows joint damage progression is unknown. Objective: To determine whether an MRI-guided treat-to-target strategy vs a conventional clinical treat-to-target strategy improves outcomes in patients with RA in clinical remission. Design, Setting, and Participants: Two-year, randomized, multicenter trial conducted at 9 hospitals in Denmark. Two hundred patients with RA in clinical remission (disease activity score in 28 joints-C-reactive protein [DAS28-CRP] <3.2 and no swollen joints) were enrolled between April 2012 and June 2015. The final follow-up visit was April 2017. Interventions: Patients were randomly allocated (1:1) to an MRI-guided vs a conventional treat-to-target strategy. In the MRI-guided group, the treatment goal was absence of MRI bone marrow edema combined with clinical remission, defined as DAS28-CRP of 3.2 or less and no swollen joints. In the conventional group, the treatment goal was clinical remission. Main Outcomes and Measures: Co-primary outcomes were proportions of patients achieving DAS28-CRP remission (DAS28-CRP <2.6) and with no radiographic progression (no increase in total van der Heijde-modified Sharp score) at 24 months. Significance testing for the primary outcome was based on 1-sided testing. Secondary outcomes were clinical and MRI measures of disease activity, physical function, and quality of life. Results: Of 200 patients randomized (133 women [67%]; mean [SD] age, 61.6 [10.5] years; median baseline DAS28-CRP, 1.9 [interquartile range, 1.7-2.2]; van der Heijde-modified Sharp score, 18.0 [interquartile range, 7.0-42.5]), 76 patients (76%) in the MRI-guided group and 95 (95%) in the conventional group completed the study. Of these, 64 (85%) vs 83 (88%), respectively, reached the primary clinical end point (risk difference, -4.8% [1-sided 95% CI, -13.6% to + ∞; 1-sided P = .19]) and 49 (66%) vs 58 (62%), respectively, reached the primary radiographic end point (risk difference, 4.7% [1-sided 95% CI, -7.0% to + ∞; 1-sided P = .25). Of 10 key secondary end points, 8 were null and 2 showed statistically significant benefit for the MRI treat-to-target group. Seventeen patients (17%) in the MRI-guided treat-to-target group and 6 patients (6%) in the conventional treat-to-target group experienced serious adverse events. Conclusions and Relevance: Among patients with RA in clinical remission, an MRI-guided treat-to-target strategy compared with a conventional treat-to-target strategy did not result in improved disease activity remission rates or reduce radiographic progression. These findings do not support the use of an MRI-guided strategy for treating patients with RA. Trial Registration: ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT01656278.","author_count":31,"contributors":["Møller-Bisgaard, Signe","Hørslev-Petersen, Kim","Ejbjerg, Bo","Hetland, Merete Lund","Ørnbjerg, Lykke Midtbøll","Glinatsi, Daniel","Møller, Jakob","Boesen, Mikael","Christensen, Robin","Stengaard-Pedersen, Kristian","Madsen, Ole Rintek","Jensen, Bente","Villadsen, Jan Alexander","Hauge, Ellen-Margrethe","Bennett, Philip","Hendricks, Oliver","Asmussen, Karsten","Kowalski, Marcin Ryszard","Lindegaard, Hanne","Nielsen, Sabrina Mai","Bliddal, Henning","Krogh, Niels Steen","Ellingsen, Torkell","Nielsen, Agnete H","Balding, Lone","Jurik, Anne Grethe","Thomsen, Henrik S","Østergaard, Mikkel"],"cuid":["S03-094.36-792911"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Møller-Bisgaard, Signe"],"keyword":["Aged","Antirheumatic Agents adverse effects","Arthritis Rheumatoid diagnostic imaging","Bone Marrow diagnostic imaging","Disease Progression","Edema diagnostic imaging","Female","Humans","Joints diagnostic imaging","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","Male","Middle Aged","Osteitis diagnostic imaging","Outcome and Process Assessment Health Care","Radiography","Remission Induction"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-OTH","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"OT":["Copenhagen Center for Arthritis Research, Center for Rheumatology and Spine Diseases, Centre for Head and Orthopaedics, Rigshospitalet, Glostrup, Denmark."],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-OTH","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effect of magnetic resonance imaging vs conventional treat to target strategies on disease activity remission and radiographic progression in rheumatoid arthritis the imagine ra randomized clinical trial","src":["aau","au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6440221/?report=printable","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-094.36-792911","identifier":[{"id":"PMC6440221","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85061060865","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30721294","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-763f272b-94b9-4c39-b5ed-b9c496bee4db","au-b6a83fce-dd1d-4e44-a878-10a43948ad7b","ku-28dfdd4b-2bd1-440a-b422-39789aae0c0e"],"keyword":["Aged","Antirheumatic Agents/adverse effects","Arthritis, Rheumatoid/diagnostic imaging","Bone Marrow/diagnostic imaging","Disease Progression","Edema/diagnostic imaging","Female","Humans","Joints/diagnostic imaging","Magnetic Resonance Imaging","Male","Middle Aged","Osteitis/diagnostic imaging","Outcome and Process Assessment (Health Care)","Radiography","Remission Induction"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-7-ADB869E992F12546085441BBEB33BCB2"],"oa_lin